Actually, let me reconsider. The `emit_writereq_of_openref` callback is called from `h2o_http2_scheduler_run`. When the stream is processed and its data is sent, `h2o_http2_stream_send_pending_data` sets `_data.size = 0` and transitions to END_STREAM. Then `emit_writereq_of_openref` checks:
```c
if (h2o_http2_stream_has_pending_data(stream) || stream->state == H2O_HTTP2_STREAM_STATE_SEND_BODY_IS_FINAL) {
    if (h2o_http2_window_get_avail(&stream->output_window) <= 0) {
        /* is blocked */
    } else {
        *still_is_active = 1;
    }
} else {
    ...
    h2o_linklist_insert(&conn->_write.streams_to_proceed, &stream->_link);
}
```

So after sending all data, the stream is added to `streams_to_proceed`. Then later, `h2o_http2_stream_proceed` is called, which closes the stream (since state is END_STREAM).

Now, when the stream is closed via `h2o_http2_stream_close`, it calls `h2o_http2_conn_unregister_stream` → `preserve_stream_scheduler`. The relocate copies `_self_is_active` from src. 

But wait - at this point, is the stream still active? The stream was active when it was processed by `emit_writereq_of_openref`. After processing, `*still_is_active` was set to 0 (since all data was sent). So the stream is deactivated in the scheduler.

